WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.466 --> 00:00:03.269
-W<00:00:00.499><c>he</c><00:00:00.532><c>n </c><00:00:00.566><c>yo</c><00:00:00.599><c>ur</c><00:00:00.633><c> f</c><00:00:00.666><c>am</c><00:00:00.699><c>il</c><00:00:00.733><c>y </c><00:00:00.766><c>le</c><00:00:00.799><c>ft</c>
<00:00:00.899><c>th</c><00:00:00.933><c>e </c><00:00:00.966><c>Wh</c><00:00:01.000><c>it</c><00:00:01.033><c>e </c><00:00:01.066><c>Ho</c><00:00:01.100><c>us</c><00:00:01.133><c>e </c><00:00:01.166><c>in</c><00:00:01.200><c> 2</c><00:00:01.233><c>00</c><00:00:01.267><c>0,</c>

00:00:03.269 --> 00:00:04.470
was there a sense of

00:00:04.470 --> 00:00:06.905
"I can't believe
we're leaving this place"

00:00:06.905 --> 00:00:09.809
or was there a sense of someone
saying, "We'll be back"?

00:00:09.809 --> 00:00:11.111
[ Laughter ]

00:00:11.111 --> 00:00:13.479
"Don't be too sad.
We'll be back one day."

00:00:13.479 --> 00:00:16.049
-Uh, no.
I don't remember that at all.

00:00:16.049 --> 00:00:19.585
Admittedly, it was in the middle
of my senior year at Stanford,

00:00:19.585 --> 00:00:20.820
so I think I was just --

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:22.722
-Whoo!
-Oh, Stanford. Yes.

00:00:22.722 --> 00:00:24.123
-There you go, yeah.

00:00:24.123 --> 00:00:26.292
-The cardinal red,
not an accident.

00:00:26.292 --> 00:00:27.994
I think I was
just really excited

00:00:27.994 --> 00:00:29.729
to go back to college,
admittedly.

00:00:29.729 --> 00:00:31.898
-Yeah, college does seem like
it might be more fun

00:00:31.898 --> 00:00:34.600
than living in the White House,
which is a strange thing to say.

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:37.737
-But when you're 20 years old,
probably not surprising.

00:00:37.737 --> 00:00:40.006
Now, I want to ask
about this too,

00:00:40.006 --> 00:00:43.709
because this campaign
that is currently happening

00:00:43.709 --> 00:00:45.178
is starting to get --

00:00:45.178 --> 00:00:47.279
I feel like we're entering
the phase of these campaigns

00:00:47.279 --> 00:00:48.280
to become a bit personal.

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:49.449
Certainly this last week,

00:00:49.449 --> 00:00:51.283
we've seen some
of the more brutal attacks

00:00:51.283 --> 00:00:52.952
from the other side.

00:00:52.952 --> 00:00:57.490
You must feel like your family
has been attacked for so long,

00:00:57.490 --> 00:00:58.358
are you --

00:00:58.358 --> 00:01:00.160
Do you feel numb to it now,

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:03.129
or is it still hard to watch
and listen to?

00:01:03.129 --> 00:01:04.931
-Well, I don't remember
a time in my life

00:01:04.931 --> 00:01:06.932
when my family
wasn't being attacked.

00:01:06.932 --> 00:01:08.334
-Mm-hmm.

00:01:08.334 --> 00:01:12.572
-And what feels different
about this election to me is

00:01:12.572 --> 00:01:14.641
I just don't remember a time

00:01:14.641 --> 00:01:17.443
when so many Americans
were being attacked

00:01:17.443 --> 00:01:20.913
by a major party's
presidential nominee.

00:01:20.913 --> 00:01:23.482
I mean, the attacks
against women,

00:01:23.482 --> 00:01:25.852
against immigrants,
against minorities,

00:01:25.852 --> 00:01:27.320
against our LGBT community,

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:29.355
against Americans
with disabilities,

00:01:29.355 --> 00:01:31.023
against a Gold Star family,
I mean.

00:01:31.023 --> 00:01:33.226
-Late-night hosts -- a couple of
them have taken it pretty hard.

00:01:33.226 --> 00:01:35.295
-I mean, really, you're just
in the cross-hairs every day.

00:01:35.295 --> 00:01:37.530
So thank you for doing that.

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:39.565
-I'm one of the heroes.
Thank you.

00:01:39.565 --> 00:01:43.369
-Well, probably if someone else
were sitting here,

00:01:43.369 --> 00:01:45.905
he might say that you're
really demonstrating courage.

00:01:45.905 --> 00:01:47.573
-Okay, yes. Exactly, exactly.

00:01:47.573 --> 00:01:51.210
-So to me,
that's what feels so different

00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:53.346
and deeply troubling, candidly.

00:01:53.346 --> 00:01:55.014
-So you're going through
this again.

00:01:55.014 --> 00:01:57.383
Another thing, which we've
actually talked about before,

00:01:57.383 --> 00:01:59.818
another thing that I feel
sympathy for you,

00:01:59.818 --> 00:02:02.588
which is I was also
a child of the '90s

00:02:02.588 --> 00:02:05.458
and I when I look back
at the way I dressed in 1992,

00:02:05.458 --> 00:02:07.594
I'm so psyched
I wasn't a public figure.

00:02:07.594 --> 00:02:09.095
[ Laughter ]

00:02:09.095 --> 00:02:12.097
-And I'm jealous
that you're not, yes.

00:02:12.097 --> 00:02:13.532
-Because let's be honest.

00:02:13.532 --> 00:02:16.102
Like, right
now you're fitting in perfectly.

00:02:16.102 --> 00:02:17.537
This look is fantastic.

00:02:17.537 --> 00:02:19.638
It was really rough in the '90s.

00:02:19.638 --> 00:02:20.940
-It was rough in the '90s.

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:23.209
-It was rough in the '90s
for all of us.

00:02:23.209 --> 00:02:25.111
-Something I was actually
thinking about today,

00:02:25.111 --> 00:02:26.312
'cause it's now October

00:02:26.312 --> 00:02:28.547
and my daughter's really excited
about Halloween

00:02:28.547 --> 00:02:29.682
and getting to dress up,

00:02:29.682 --> 00:02:32.051
is I remember
convincing my mother

00:02:32.051 --> 00:02:35.188
to let me put on the orange and
black rubber bands on my braces.

00:02:35.188 --> 00:02:36.422
-Yeah.

00:02:36.422 --> 00:02:38.725
-And her actually
trying to tell me,

00:02:38.725 --> 00:02:41.561
like, I wasn't gonna be back
in the orthodontist for weeks.

00:02:41.561 --> 00:02:42.961
[ Laughter ]

00:02:42.961 --> 00:02:44.764
Although maybe that was
a fun thing to do for Halloween,

00:02:44.764 --> 00:02:46.232
it was probably gonna be
a decision I regretted.

00:02:46.232 --> 00:02:47.767
And sure enough,
like November 1st I was like,

00:02:47.767 --> 00:02:49.435
"Mom, can we go back
to the orthodontist?"

00:02:49.435 --> 00:02:50.903
And she was like,
"No, we cannot."

00:02:50.903 --> 00:02:56.509
So not only are
my '90s fashion expressions --

00:02:56.509 --> 00:02:59.011
misguided foibles --

00:02:59.011 --> 00:03:00.580
recorded for eternity,

00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:02.181
so too are my braces colors.

00:03:02.181 --> 00:03:03.282
-Yeah, there you go.

00:03:03.282 --> 00:03:04.817
-Which is also just really sad.

00:03:04.817 --> 00:03:06.051
-Look, it shows you really love

00:03:06.051 --> 00:03:07.754
and are enthusiastic
about the holidays,

00:03:07.754 --> 00:03:09.154
which I think
says a lot about you.

00:03:09.154 --> 00:03:11.190
-Thank you. So hopefully my
children will feel the same way.

00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:13.793
-And you mentioned your --
So your daughter is 2?

00:03:13.793 --> 00:03:15.294
-Yes.

00:03:15.294 --> 00:03:18.665
-And is it true that you read
the newspaper to her every day?

00:03:18.665 --> 00:03:20.132
-I read the headlines to her

00:03:20.132 --> 00:03:22.268
and to her little brother,
Aidan, who's 3 1/2 months.

00:03:22.268 --> 00:03:23.703
-Seth: 3 1/2 months, gotcha.

00:03:23.703 --> 00:03:26.105
And is that a something that
your parents did for you, yes?

00:03:26.105 --> 00:03:28.007
-The first thing I remember
learning how to read

00:03:28.007 --> 00:03:29.308
is the newspaper.

00:03:29.308 --> 00:03:32.945
And we would talk about
kind of what I was reading

00:03:32.945 --> 00:03:35.081
over breakfast or over dinner,

00:03:35.081 --> 00:03:38.050
and so I hope to have that same
tradition with my children.

00:03:38.050 --> 00:03:39.384
And I realize

00:03:39.384 --> 00:03:40.820
they don't quite understand
what I'm talking about,

00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:43.556
but hopefully it'll just be
part of their lives

00:03:43.556 --> 00:03:46.793
so that when they do understand,
they will have opinions.

00:03:46.793 --> 00:03:48.695
And I'm just so excited to learn

00:03:48.695 --> 00:03:51.263
what they're gonna be
most curious about,

00:03:51.263 --> 00:03:53.465
interested in,
and angry about the world.

00:03:53.465 --> 00:03:55.801
-Are there some headlines with,
based on the fact

00:03:55.801 --> 00:03:57.603
how much their grandmother
is in the news, you think,

00:03:57.603 --> 00:03:59.472
"I might leave that one out,
I might skip that one"?

00:03:59.472 --> 00:04:03.075
-Well, I don't read the things

00:04:03.075 --> 00:04:05.444
that I don't think
children should be hearing.

00:04:05.444 --> 00:04:06.479
-Gotcha. That's cool.

00:04:06.479 --> 00:04:07.880
-Of any age.

00:04:07.880 --> 00:04:10.015
-Yeah, with the newspaper,
you're probably like,

00:04:10.015 --> 00:04:13.152
"Page 1, 2, 8, 19, 20,
there we go 25."

00:04:13.152 --> 00:04:15.721
-Yeah, so there is definitely
like selective reading.

00:04:15.721 --> 00:04:18.057
-"Oh, there's a new train line.
Let's read you this one."

